in other news tonight a henderson woman  is being recognized for her work  advocating for abused and neglected  children she's a court-appointed special  advocate also known as casa joe muller  introduces us to carolyn maskari who has  a message for others about this program  i've always believed that the only  important thing in the world are people  with walls full of family photos and the  most vulnerable are children it's  obvious carolyn maskari loves her family  our job is to advocate for their best  interests for four decades mascari has  shared her love with more than just her  family i always tell everybody it's the  best paying job i ever had but not in  money she has been a casa for 40 years  that is a court appointed special  advocate and what we do is advocate for  children in the foster care system who  have been abused or neglected  in the court we have we advocate for  them in the court to do what is in their  best interest she is the longest serving  casa in the country and because of that  work the national organization  recognized her at a ceremony in seattle  when i heard about it i was kind of  blown away to tell you the truth there  are 90 over 93 000 classes nationwide  and i've been the longest serving she  says it was bittersweet winning the  award we were married for 57 years her  husband was her biggest supporter he  always was very very supportive very  encouraging but he passed away months  ago he made me to be what i was able to  do i felt like i couldn't have done it  without the support of my family now she  has a message for the rest of us i just  really would like to see us get more  cautious we just really really  need more causes she hopes more people  can volunteer their time  don't plan to ever stop joe muller live  local now for more information on how to  volunteer visit our website eight news  now dot
 

